// Copy this file to Secrets.swift and fill in your credentials.
// Secrets.swift is gitignored (.gitignore: **/Secrets.swift) — never commit it.
//
// supabaseAnon — the anon (publishable) key. Safe to embed: it's the same key
// the web apps ship. It grants READ ONLY on this user's rows, via the
// widget_anon_read RLS policies. It has NO write access to any pf_/protocol_
// table — that was deliberately revoked.
// Get it from: Supabase Dashboard → Project Settings → API → anon public key
//
// userID — the user_id your rows are stored under. TimeTracker uses "default";
// PathFinder + Protocol use your Supabase auth uid — use that uid for pf_/protocol_ data.
//
// widgetKey — a DEDICATED single-purpose secret, NOT the anon key. It is only
// accepted by the `habit-toggle` Edge Function, which can do exactly one thing:
// toggle this user's habit completion. It grants no read access to anything.
// It must match the WIDGET_HABIT_KEY secret on the Supabase project:
//     supabase secrets set WIDGET_HABIT_KEY=<value> --project-ref efxmzsdisaymtpebaxlp
// Rotate by setting a new value there and rebuilding the widget with it here.
//
// widgetSessionKey — a SECOND dedicated single-purpose secret, also NOT the anon
// key and deliberately NOT the same value as widgetKey. It is only accepted by
// the `session-toggle` Edge Function, which can do exactly two things: start and
// stop this user's time-tracking session. It grants no read access to anything.
// Separate keys mean each one's blast radius stays its own — extracting the
// habit key from the binary must not also hand over the timer, and either can be
// rotated without disturbing the other. It must match WIDGET_SESSION_KEY:
//     supabase secrets set WIDGET_SESSION_KEY=<value> --project-ref efxmzsdisaymtpebaxlp
// Both keys must be at least 32 characters — both functions fail closed below
// that rather than accepting a stubby or empty credential.
//
// Why a separate key at all: a widget extension on a free-tier sideloaded
// install can't receive the user's JWT (App Groups need paid provisioning), so
// it cannot authenticate as the user. Shipping a scoped, revocable secret keeps
// the blast radius at "can toggle habits" instead of "can write anything the
// public anon key can reach". It is still extractable from the binary — that's
// inherent to any credential shipped in an app, and the reason the function
// validates ownership server-side rather than trusting the caller.
enum Secrets {
    static let supabaseURL       = "https://efxmzsdisaymtpebaxlp.supabase.co"
    static let supabaseAnon      = "PASTE_YOUR_ANON_KEY_HERE"
    static let userID            = "YOUR_SUPABASE_AUTH_UID"
    static let widgetKey         = "PASTE_YOUR_WIDGET_HABIT_KEY_HERE"
    static let widgetSessionKey  = "PASTE_YOUR_WIDGET_SESSION_KEY_HERE"
}
